  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 open-access scholarly book is already showing signs of active use, with 468 recorded downloads and a clear alignment with the SDG area of Peace, Justice and strong institutions. The record also places it within a policy-relevant humanities and art context, supported by KU Leuven funding and an openly accessible publication route.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">La Monarqu\u00eda Indiana de Carlos II en la encrujiada examines political change in late seventeenth-century Spanish America, focusing on the Viceroyalty of Peru and the reforms associated with Carlos II. The book\u2019s framing emphasizes that Spanish America was not simply a story of imperial decline; instead, it presents the region as an early site of administrative experimentation and transformation in the viceroyal courts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is usage: the book has attracted 468 downloads, with activity spread across the year and noticeable peaks in March and December. That pattern suggests sustained interest rather than a single brief surge, consistent with a work that is being consulted by readers engaging with colonial, political, and cultural history.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication is openly available through an open-access channel, which likely supports that reach, and it is associated with DOAB and OpenAIRE indexing. It is also marked as peer-reviewed in the publisher\u2019s open-science workflow, and the funding record notes support from KU Leuven. Together, these signals point to a formally supported scholarly monograph with broad accessibility.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record is still at an early stage, with no citations recorded in OpenAlex yet. That makes the book\u2019s scholarly uptake difficult to judge in citation terms, so the present story is one of readership and availability rather than bibliometric accumulation. Public discourse data are not available in the archive, and no teaching adoption evidence is recorded beyond a WorldCat holdings signal.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the book\u2019s impact profile is best understood as emerging and dissemination-oriented: it is visible as an accessible, peer-reviewed monograph in the humanities, with concrete download activity and thematic relevance to governance, institutions, and historical change. The record supports the view that the work is reaching readers now, while longer-term scholarly influence has yet to develop in the citation data.</p>",
